# test/KNOWN_DEFECTS - what this toolkit does not do, written down where the
# suite lives rather than in somebody's head.
#
# Two kinds of entry, and the difference matters:
#
#   DEFECT     an assertion that is CORRECT and that the toolkit does not
#              satisfy. Where the suite can express it, it is marked with
#              t_known_defect - which is NOT red today, and goes RED the moment
#              it starts passing, because a defect marker that outlives its
#              defect is how a suite starts lying about what it covers.
#
#   UNPROVEN   the toolkit may well be right; nothing has ever demonstrated it.
#              These are not failures. They are the difference between "tested"
#              and "believed", and the reason to write them here is that a green
#              suite is otherwise indistinguishable from a complete one.
#
# Adding a line here is not a way to make something go quiet. A DEFECT line is a
# statement that the toolkit is wrong, with the fix written next to it. An
# UNPROVEN line is a statement about the limits of the evidence, with what would
# settle it written next to it.
