## mk/flow.mk: 1771 lines, and no recipe that launches a tool is tested
# CORRECTED 2026-09-22. This entry used to read "1709 lines, and only its
# parse-time guards are tested", and to say that nothing covered "the stage
# graph, the ~70-variable surface, the ordering of the `?=` chains, or any
# recipe". All four of those are now wrong, and the line count was wrong too.
# test/shell/t_flow_mk.sh plants 69 faults across exactly that surface: it
# reads the stage graph out of `make -n`, the resolved values out of
# `make --eval`, the export set out of a throwaway target that reports $(VAR)
# and $$VAR in one process, and the rule database out of `make -p`.
#
# WHAT IS STILL TRUE: the recipes it runs are the ones that run with no tool.
# No assertion here has ever watched a stage recipe actually launch Vivado, so
# what the rules DO once a tool is in the loop rests on the integration result
# and nothing else.
#
# THE SPLIT IS NOW UNBLOCKED, AND STILL A JUDGEMENT CALL. The old entry made
# the suite the precondition - "a suite that drives `make -n` over the stage
# graph and asserts the prerequisite chain and the resolved variable surface;
# THEN the split. Not the other way round." That precondition is met, and the
# suite's own header says so: every line a split would move passes the same
# three hazards that bit this file in its first fortnight (a `:=` snapshotted
# before the includes ran, a target expanded at parse time to the filesystem
# root, an `$(if)` whose prose contained a comma), and each of those is now
# planted and rejected. What has not changed is the payoff: still no functional
# gain, on the one component with demonstrated bitstream parity.

